{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Espoa5XUQCdqb9APWguKxz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research data carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey findings from the data:\n\n**Claim 1 (KL vs BCE):**\n- KL 50% PPL: ~9.15-9.25 (5 seeds, mean ~9.19)\n- BCE 50% PPL: ~27.96-28.38 (3 seeds)\n- BCE iso-compute (2700 steps) PPL: ~27.97 (saturated)\n- TEAL 50% PPL: 23.31\n- KL advantage is massive and structural\n\n\n- When BCE is trained on KL-derived oracle masks, it achieves PPL 9.29 with 0.978 oracle agreement, though the masks diverge 53% from magnitude-based oracles—this reveals the core pattern discovery mechanism\n\n**Claim 2 (SPON subsumption):**\n- SPON recovery is negligible at 7.3% with near-orthogonal cosine similarity of 0.196\n- Magnitude ratio of 15.8% confirms SPON doesn't subsume the learned representations\n\n**Claim 3 (Phase transition):**\n- At 30% uniform masking, MMLU jumps to 40.6% while variance drops 7.5×, indicating a sharp transition point\n- Performance degrades non-monotonically: 40% uniform shows global collapse, 50% uniform drops both MMLU (38.1 vs 30.7) and performance metrics further, confirming the non-monotonic allocation preference\n\n**Scaling to 14B (Qwen):**\n- KL maintains its PPL advantage (7.62 vs TEAL's 8.73) but MMLU shows a significant gap favoring TEAL (71.83 vs KL's 63.72-64.49)\n- Uniform masking doesn't resolve this discrepancy\n\n**Mistral results:**\n- KL preserves its PPL advantage across sparsity levels, though downstream evaluation remains incomplete\n\nSeveral experiments are still running, including various sparsity configurations and ablation evaluations, while LongBench has encountered repeated failures.\n\nOn the practical side, there's no actual wall-clock speedup without sparse kernels. Now I'm thinking about how to frame this work around the mask pattern discovery angle — the key insight being that BCE applied to KL targets yields good perplexity, suggesting KL discovers superior masks, with the 53% mask divergence as a novel empirical finding. The phase transition and regime analysis also seem central to the narrative.\n\nThe main uncertainties are whether the remaining experiments will support this framing, how reviewers will interpret the 14B MMLU gap, whether the lack of wall-clock speedup becomes a liability, and whether the mask pattern discovery framing is compelling enough to counter an \"incremental\" critique.\n\nFor the reception scenarios: best case has the C1 ablation confirming mask quality at uniform allocation, clean regime boundaries in the sweep, strong KL advantage at the recommended operating point, and reviewers embracing the discovery framing. Median case involves noisier results and requests for deeper analysis, with the 14B gap flagged as a limitation but the work still competitive. Worst case is if the PPL gains come from sparsity mismatch rather than mask quality, the sweep lacks clean structure, and reviewers see the work as incremental despite the reframing — which would require finding a new angle entirely.\n\nNow I'm defining the specific metrics that will determine which scenario plays out: the C1 ablation result on uniform allocation, the BCE 30% baseline performance, how cleanly the 6-point sweep shows regime transitions, whether Mistral shows consistent patterns, and ultimately what reviewer scores come back. I'm setting concrete thresholds for each — like C1 needing PPL around 9.5 to confirm mask quality is the driver, BCE 30% needing to underperform significantly, and the sweep needing a clear transition point around 30-35% to support the practical guideline. on scale)\n- Narrative: \"mask pattern discovery\" fully validated, all three claims hold\n\n**Median:**\n- C1 ablation uniform eval: PPL 9.5-11.0 (some gap vs KL, suggesting mask quality matters but isn't the whole story)\n- BCE 30%: PPL 10-12, moderate KL advantage\n- 6-point sweep: regime exists but boundaries are noisy\n- Average reviewer score: 3.0-3.5\n- Narrative: need to soften \"discovery\" language, add caveats about scale dependence\n\n**Worst:**\n- C1 ablation uniform eval: PPL > 11 (mask quality alone doesn't explain KL advantage)\n- BCE 30%: PPL 9-10 (small gap vs KL at 30%)\n- 6-point sweep: no clean transition\n- Average reviewer score: < 3.0\n- Narrative: need complete reframe\n\nWait, I should reconsider this. The C1 ablation already has results — PPL 9.29 at 60.9% sparsity with 97.8% oracle agreement, which is strong evidence that BCE can learn KL-derived masks effectively. The real gap is whether this holds when I evaluate at uniform 50% sparsity instead of the predictor's native 60.9%, and what the other experiments show.\n\nFor the paper deadline, I need to focus on which experiments actually complete and what they tell me about whether the mask pattern narrative holds up. The key metrics are the C1 uniform evaluation at 50%, the BCE 30% baseline to fill the table, and the 6-point sweep for granularity. Looking at the current numbers — KL at 50% is around 11.79 PPL, BCE at 50% is around 27.97 — I need to see where the remaining results land to know if the story is coherent.\n\nThe C1 ablation with KL targets runs natively at 60.9% sparsity with 9.29 PPL, but when I force it to 50% via TEAL top-k matching, the sparsity constraint should push PPL down into the 8.5–10.5 range. I've got KL at 30% around 8.82 and TEAL at 30% around 10.97, but BCE at 30% is likely to be substantially worse — probably 15–25 range given how much BCE struggles compared to KL. The best case is if all these experiments confirm that mask quality is the dominant factor, the 6-point sweep shows clean boundaries, and BCE consistently underperforms, which would make the paper narrative tight and convincing.\n\nIn a median outcome, the C1 ablation would show that mask quality matters but there's still a gap suggesting training dynamics play a role too, the sweep would add data without crisp regime boundaries, and I'd need to soften the framing from \"discovery\" to \"mask patterns contribute significantly\" while acknowledging that the optimization process itself matters. Worst case is if the ablation shows the gap persists even at matched sparsity, pointing to the continuous KL optimization being the real advantage rather than the masks alone, which would shift the paper from a methods contribution to an empirical characterization study.\n\nNow I'm setting up the YAML with specific number ranges, focusing on what the C1 ablation at uniform sparsity will show.\n\nI'm thinking through which metrics actually matter: the C1 ablation PPL at 50% sparsity tells me whether mask quality alone explains the KL advantage, the BCE performance at the recommended operating point shows the practical cost of the simpler approach, the 14B MMLU gap indicates whether the scaling story holds, how sharp the regime transition is matters for the narrative, and the reviewer score estimate anchors expectations.\n\nBut wait—the 14B experiments are already locked in, so I should focus on what's still genuinely uncertain. The real unknowns are the C1 ablation result with uniform masking, the BCE PPL at 30%, how well a piecewise model captures the 6-point sweep, and the overall paper score. I also need to account for Mistral downstream work that's incomplete and experiments still in preparation.\n\nPractically, I should anchor the tiers around the two core uncertainties: how BCE performs when matched to KL targets at 50% sparsity, and how it performs at the recommended operating point.\n\nDefining tiers around whether the C1 ablation cleanly validates the mask pattern story, whether the 6-point sweep shows clear regime boundaries, whether BCE significantly underperforms KL, and whether all remaining runs finish on schedule. I'm reconsidering the ablation numbers for the 50% sparsity case—if forcing lower sparsity via TEAL top-k should improve perplexity, then the PPL range might actually be 8.5-10.5, which would make BCE-on-KL-targets perform better than expected at that operating point. than KL at 50%? That seems unlikely. Let me think...\n\nThe C1 ablation predictor was trained with BCE loss on KL-derived targets. At its native output, it predicts ~61% sparsity and gets 9.29 PPL. If we apply TEAL top-k at 50% (keeping fewer neurons sparse than the predictor wants), that means we're keeping MORE neurons active, so PPL should improve from 9.29.\n\nBut wait — the KL predictor at 50% gets ~11.8 PPL via TEAL global. So would BCE-on-KL-targets at 50% be better than KL at 50%? That seems paradoxical.\n\nActually, the C1 ablation predictor's native sparsity is 60.9%, and its PPL there is 9.29. The KL predictor at 50% gets ~9.15-9.25 per-token with a hard mask, but ~11.8 when using TEAL global, which redistributes sparsity across layers differently. When I apply TEAL global top-k to the C1 predictor's scores at 50%, there's a mismatch between what it was trained to predict (~50%) and what it actually predicts (~61%), which could swing the results either way. I'm probably overthinking this — let me just compare the actual numbers: KL gives ~9.15-9.25 per-token PPL at 50%, while C1 ablation sits at 9.29 at its native 60.9% sparsity. 50% = ?\n\nThe TEAL global PPL is typically worse than per-token because global reallocation isn't optimal for each token. So C1 ablation at TEAL 50% might be around 10-13 range.\n\nFor best case, c1_ablation at TEAL 50% ≈ KL at TEAL 50% (~11.8) would confirm mask quality is sufficient.\nFor median, c1_ablation at TEAL 50% is 12-15, suggesting mask quality explains most but not all.\nFor worst, c1_ablation at TEAL 50% > 15, suggesting mask quality alone is insufficient.\n\nI should focus on the uniform allocation evaluation for C1 ablation at 50% as my key metric. Now I'm thinking through the main uncertainties: whether the mask pattern narrative holds up under standardized comparison, how bad BCE performs at 30%, whether the intermediate sweep points fill in cleanly, and whether reviewers find the reframed narrative around mask pattern discovery compelling. For the venue, I'm considering the ARR scale where 4+ is strong accept, 3.5 is borderline accept, 3.0 is borderline, 2.5 is borderline reject, and below that is reject territory.\n\nGiven the strong empirical results but acknowledged limitations like the lack of speedup and the 14B gap, I'm estimating best case at 3.5-4.0 for acceptance, median around 3.0-3.5 as borderline needing revision, and worst case at 2.0-3.0 for rejection requiring major changes. The mask divergence finding at 53% is already measured, but in the best case it's positioned as a novel discovery while in the worst case reviewers dismiss it as insufficient evidence that the discovered masks are actually better. Now I'm structuring the final outcomes: best case is when C1 ablation confirms mask quality is sufficient, all remaining experiments land cleanly, the reframe feels compelling, and the paper gets accepted as-is.\n\nFor the median scenario, C1 ablation shows mask quality matters but isn't fully sufficient—the residual gap suggests optimization dynamics play a role too—so I'd need to soften the \"discovery\" framing and introduce \"optimization quality\" as a contributing factor, landing in borderline accept territory with possible ARR revision. In the worst case, either the C1 ablation gap is too large and undermines the core reframe, or key experiments don't finish in time, making reviewers see it as incremental. Even then, since the goal is to stay with EMNLP Main, I'd pivot the paper toward \"comprehensive empirical study\" or \"practical guidelines\" rather than pure mask discovery, but still targeting the main conference.\n\nNow I'm clarifying the ARR scoring system—a 4.0 target maps to the top 15-20% tier for main conference acceptance, while 3.0-3.5 typically lands in Findings or needs revision, and anything below 2.5-3.0 requires major repositioning to still have a shot at Main.\n\nGiven the strong empirical results, hitting that 4.0 target is ambitious but realistic. Let me work through the ablation metrics more carefully—I need to establish the right comparison baselines for the uniform mask quality experiments and see how the performance gaps actually break down across the best, median, and worst-case scenarios. ] — nearly matches KL at 30%, undermining KL advantage at operating point.\n\nI need to reconsider whether BCE at 30% would even be viable. BCE was trained at 50% and struggled to maintain sparsity through the Lagrangian, so training a fresh model at 30% is uncertain. Looking at the registry, bce_30pct_s42 finished but has no metrics, while bce_30pct_s42_eval is still preparing. Given BCE's sparsity control issues at 14B where it collapsed to 6.4%, there could be training problems here too. That said, at 8B with 50% sparsity BCE trains fine and hits ~28 PPL, so at 30% with a less aggressive target it should work — the real question is how well it performs.\n\nThe gap between BCE and KL at 50% is substantial: ~28 PPL versus ~11.8, a 2.37× ratio. At 30% sparsity both methods are closer to dense, so that ratio might compress. KL hits 8.82 at 30%, so if the ratio held BCE would land around 20 PPL. But lower sparsity means BCE's per-neuron independence assumption matters less, potentially improving its relative performance. I'm estimating three scenarios: best case BCE stays above 13 PPL confirming KL's advantage, median case lands between 10-13 PPL showing KL still wins but less decisively, or worst case drops below 10 PPL which would weaken KL's edge at the recommended operating point.\n\nNow I'm checking the seed consistency across KL 30% uniform MMLU runs. The registry shows three measurements: 0.4061, 0.3763, and 0.3727, which is a pretty wide spread with a mean around 0.385 and standard deviation near 0.018. Comparing this to TEAL's single 30% measurement of 0.3861, the difference between TEAL and KL is basically within noise.\n\nBut I'm noticing something odd—there are also global_eval and uniform_eval variants for s456 that show 0.4382 and 0.4198 respectively, which don't match the v2 uniform result of 0.3727. These must be using different predictor training runs, which explains the variance. The v2 versions had a different training setup, so this inconsistency is worth flagging as a key uncertainty for the downstream estimates.\n\nFor the tier definitions, I need to capture what clean versus noisy transitions look like. The best case would show a clear piecewise structure with visible transitions at the 25%/35%/45% boundaries, the median case would have noisy data but a discernible trend, and the worst case would show no clear transition at all with everything collapsing onto a single curve.\n\nNow I'm ready to draft the comprehensive YAML structure that captures all these tier definitions and their underlying assumptions. Looking at the expected performance ranges and narrative direction, the C1 ablation provides solid evidence that the mask pattern discovery framing holds up—the KL advantage stems from finding better sparse patterns rather than just training signal coupling, and recovering KL-level quality with BCE on KL targets plus the 53% mask divergence gives us a strong causal chain. The phase transition insight adds novelty without requiring any claim adjustments, so I can present all three contributions as planned.\n\nHowever, there are some experimental uncertainties I need to account for: the BCE-on-KL-targets results sit in a middle ground between vanilla BCE and full KL performance, some of the sweep points show noise with regime boundaries fuzzy within a few percentage points, and the downstream variance across seeds is larger than ideal, making individual results less stable. The 14B MMLU gap could be flagged as a weakness by reviewers, so I should be prepared to address that.\n\nNow looking at the next concerns: at least one reviewer might question whether the novelty is sufficient given that KL distillation is already well-established. I'm also seeing specific number ranges for the key metrics—the ablation results, the forced top-k performance, the variance across seeds, and the regime fitting quality—which I should use to ground my claims. The narrative needs adjustment too: I should frame the mask pattern discovery more modestly as a significant contributing factor rather than the main finding, and the C1 ablation suggests mask quality explains a substantial portion of the performance variation.\n\nThe core issue is that while the ablation shows mask quality accounts for roughly 60-75% of the KL advantage, there's still a meaningful residual gap that points to continuous optimization also playing an independent role. So the contribution should be reframed as KL improving both mask quality and optimization dynamics, with mask quality being the dominant factor. I might need to add an ablation that cleanly separates these two effects to strengthen the claim. The phase transition finding could be elevated to a co-equal contribution to boost overall novelty. There's a real risk the paper lands in Findings rather than Main track, which would require a revision cycle to address.\n\nThe worst-case scenario is that the ablation doesn't actually validate the mask pattern narrative—either the residual gap is too large or points in the wrong direction, requiring a complete reframing. This could happen if BCE applied to KL targets at 50% uniform PPL stays above 15.0 (nearly matching vanilla BCE), suggesting mask quality isn't the primary driver of KL's gains. Or if BCE at 30% PPL drops below 10.0 and stays close to KL's performance, that would undermine KL's unique value at the recommended operating point. The downstream variance across seeds is also concerning—if MMLU shows σ > 4 percentage points, the headline numbers become seed-dependent. The 6-point sweep might reveal continuous degradation rather than a true phase transition, invalidating the regime narrative entirely. Multiple reviewers could also flag fundamental issues with the framing or experimental design. I'm considering two major pivots to salvage this work for EMNLP. The first frames the paper as a practical deployment guide—turning it into a comprehensive empirical study of how sparsity, quality, and allocation interact, with actionable recommendations for practitioners on which methods to use when. The second angle focuses on loss landscape analysis, positioning KL's advantage as stemming from better optimization properties rather than mask quality, elevating the gradient SNR findings to the main narrative and reframing this as theoretical work on why KL distillation outperforms BCE for sparse prediction. Both directions require substantial rewrites of the methodology section and reordering the experiments, but the underlying data is already there to support either approach.\n\nNow I'm verifying the consistency of my metrics across different configurations—checking that best, median, and worst values maintain the expected ordering for each metric, accounting for whether higher or lower values are preferable in each case. I need to clarify the directionality for BCE at 30% sparsity—higher PPL means a bigger gap versus KL, which strengthens the paper's narrative. But there's a tension here: if BCE performs too well at 30%, it undermines the argument for why KL is the better choice at that operating point, unless KL still maintains a clear advantage at higher sparsity levels.\n\nI'm also considering that BCE at 30% is a fresh training run with its own sparsity control challenges, so it might struggle like it did at 14B, or it might work fine like it did at 50% on 8B. Either way, the ranges I've settled on feel defensible.\n\nThe narrative arc across tiers is coming together nicely—moving from \"mask quality is the explanation\" at best, to \"mask quality is one factor\" at median, to \"mask quality isn't the main driver\" at worst, which creates a natural spectrum that matches the numeric transitions.\n\nNow I'm writing out the final YAML output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33975,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33975},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ab4d9b8-e1b8-4897-8844-58d1e492e616","uuid":"ac6c320f-15ad-401d-9fdf-f91f02a41d28"}
